.he "tar (1)                      8/16/26                      tar (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
tar
- tape archiver
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
tar -key [ name ... ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Tar saves and restores files on a single archive, usually a tape but
any file will do.  Its action is set by the key, a string of
characters holding one function letter and any number of modifiers.
The remaining arguments are file or directory names; a directory
stands for the files and subdirectories under it, recursively.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
r	Append the named files to the archive.

.ti -10
x	Extract the named files, or the whole archive if none are named.

.ti -10
t	List the named files, or the whole archive.

.ti -10
c	Create a new archive; implies r.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
MODIFIERS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
f file	Use file as the archive; - means the standard output or input.  The default is /dev/mt0.

.ti -10
b n	Use a blocking factor of n 512-byte blocks; the default is 20.

.ti -10
v	List each file as it is treated.

.ti -10
w	Ask before each file; a y answer goes ahead.

.ti -10
l	Complain about links that cannot all be resolved.

.ti -10
o	Leave directory entries out of the archive.

.ti -10
p	Restore files to their original modes, ignoring the umask.

.ti -10
i	Ignore checksum errors on directories.

.ti -10
B	Force blocking to twenty blocks a record.

.ti -10
F	Skip SCCS and RCS directories and core and errs files; given twice, also .o and a.out.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The port dropped the u flag, which leaned on sort and awk, and the h
flag along with symbolic links, which this machine does not have;
extracting an entry that is a symbolic link prints a complaint and
moves on.  Modification times are not restored, since there is no
utime to restore them with.  File names are limited to 100
characters, and the default archive is /dev/mt0, not /dev/rmt?.
.sp
A name argument of -C directory changes to that directory for the
names that follow it when writing.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ar (1), cp (1)
